WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.290
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.438 --> 00:00:07.980
SEAN GINEVAN: My
name's Sean Ginevan.

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:11.160
I work on the Android Enterprise
Business team at Google.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:15.270
Our team is responsible for the
business-to-business success

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:16.830
of the Android platform.

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:21.720
We're a dedicated team of
engineers, product managers,

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:24.432
go-to-market folks, like
myself, who are ultimately

00:00:24.432 --> 00:00:25.890
trying to make sure
that Android is

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:28.500
a great platform
for your employees

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:31.920
and for business transformation
projects that your company may

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:32.700
want to do.

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:34.590
And what I wanted to
do is give a little bit

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:37.770
of a history of some of the work
we've been doing on the Android

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:38.800
platform.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:42.060
But more importantly,
introduce some marketing

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:46.560
go-to-market initiatives that
we've launched here at Google

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:50.100
to hopefully make understanding
how to adopt Android

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:54.150
into your organization easier
so you can focus on real things

00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:58.300
like what you want to do
inside of your business.

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:02.550
From a employee enterprise
enablement perspective,

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:04.650
we've done a ton of
technical investment

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:06.690
in Android, going
all the way back

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:08.690
to the days of Android Lollipop.

00:01:08.690 --> 00:01:12.060
In Android 5, we introduced
this concept of Android

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:15.510
for Work and two core features.

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:18.240
One which was the idea of
the work profile, which

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:22.170
helped organizations who wanted
to support BYOD, allow end

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:25.440
users to bring their own devices
in a way that would be secure,

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:28.170
such that basically
data applications

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:31.020
that you deploy to
that end user wouldn't

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:34.112
interact with
personal information

00:01:34.112 --> 00:01:35.070
that the user may have.

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:36.840
And more importantly,
that was designed

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:37.980
to protect user privacy.

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:41.090
We wanted to make sure that
IT wasn't snooping on the file

00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:44.880
system, the photos, the music,
anything else the user may

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:46.660
have brought with them.

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:49.500
We also, in parallel,
introduced features

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:51.100
for fully managed devices.

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:54.630
This was the idea that if
you're of thinking about things

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:57.240
in the old BlackBerry
world, the idea

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:01.140
that I can lock down the device,
the idea that I can whitelist

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:02.790
the applications that
go on that device

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:05.220
and have a relatively
full degree of control

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:07.110
over the hardware.

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:09.330
In every version
since Android 5,

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:11.970
we've done more
technical investment

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:13.650
into the Android platform.

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:16.800
We introduced better
support for what

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:18.990
we call corporate-owned
single-use or dedicated

00:02:18.990 --> 00:02:19.583
devices.

00:02:19.583 --> 00:02:21.000
These are the
barcode scanners you

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.520
see at places like
Walmart, offering

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:26.850
higher degree of control to make
sure that device is used only

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:29.040
for barcode scanning
and not, say, watching

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:30.673
YouTube on the job.

00:02:30.673 --> 00:02:33.090
We introduced better support
for things like certificates.

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:36.660
And carrying on to Nougat, we
introduced improved usability

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:39.160
controls, like the
work profile challenge.

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:44.340
So that way, if I go through
and allow my nephew to go

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:47.550
and play with my device and
I turn off the work profile

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:50.160
so that way my nephew doesn't
play with my email account,

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:52.030
not that that
would ever happen--

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:56.130
the challenge with the passcode
before those applications

00:02:56.130 --> 00:02:57.390
can be accessed.

00:02:57.390 --> 00:03:00.000
We've introduced
features most recently

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:02.550
in Oreo called zero-touch.

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:04.590
This allows an
enterprise to make sure

00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:08.190
that, as you unbox the device,
that device automatically

00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:10.155
gets enrolled in for
management, automatically

00:03:10.155 --> 00:03:12.030
gets provisioned with
corporate applications.

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:15.780
We're really elevating the
experience and activation

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:18.270
process that an end user
needs to go through.

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:20.040
And now, with Android
Pie, we've really

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:22.920
focused on a lot of
those frontline workers

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:25.980
that we've talked about
here at Cloud Next,

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:29.040
improving things like
shared device use cases,

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:31.650
improving things like
support for multiple users,

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:34.560
and improving a lot of the
UX around the work profile,

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:37.380
making it easier to find
the applications that IT

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:39.480
has provisioned out to you.

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:42.150
To sort of up-level a lot
of what this looks like,

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:44.447
if you haven't adopted
Android Enterprise-- actually,

00:03:44.447 --> 00:03:45.280
quick show of hands.

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:47.610
How many folks are actually
running Android inside

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:49.640
of your organization?

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:50.140
OK.

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:52.098
I think I can almost see
through the spotlights

00:03:52.098 --> 00:03:53.230
and the projector.

00:03:53.230 --> 00:03:56.100
How many of you are
running Android Enterprise?

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:56.600
OK.

00:03:56.600 --> 00:03:57.710
Only a couple of you.

00:03:57.710 --> 00:04:00.300
We'll talk about why that's
important in a minute.

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:02.282
But when you look at
the Android platform

00:04:02.282 --> 00:04:03.740
and what you're
able to do with it,

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:07.010
it really allows you a
high degree of flexibility

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:09.450
when you start to
go and do things

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:10.700
that are more than just email.

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:13.540
Frankly, even if you are
just doing just email.

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:16.730
The personally-owned
world makes it such

00:04:16.730 --> 00:04:20.390
that IT has very limited
control over what

00:04:20.390 --> 00:04:22.070
you can do with that device.

00:04:22.070 --> 00:04:25.130
You can only view and manage
the work applications,

00:04:25.130 --> 00:04:28.280
you have very little
control over the device,

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:29.510
you can provision Wi-Fi.

00:04:29.510 --> 00:04:30.410
That's about it.

00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:31.755
VPN, that's about it.

00:04:31.755 --> 00:04:33.380
And there's a high
degree of separation

00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:35.090
between work and personal.

00:04:35.090 --> 00:04:37.100
At a technical level,
what we're actually doing

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:38.690
is creating a
separate user space

00:04:38.690 --> 00:04:41.690
on that device in the
work profile world.

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:45.560
It means that it's almost like
having two instances of Android

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:47.030
running on the same device.

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:48.740
File systems are
handled separately,

00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:52.700
processes are isolated,
memory is isolated, even

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:55.590
cryptographically, the data
can be stored different.

00:04:55.590 --> 00:04:57.980
And so it makes
sure that there's

00:04:57.980 --> 00:05:00.230
a high degree of separation
between work and personal.

00:05:00.230 --> 00:05:03.020
I can't just click open in
and share random data out

00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:04.730
to Facebook.

00:05:04.730 --> 00:05:06.830
On the company's
own side of things,

00:05:06.830 --> 00:05:09.470
you enforce full control
over that device.

00:05:09.470 --> 00:05:10.940
As I mentioned
earlier, that could

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:13.860
be as simple as whitelisting
what goes on that device.

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:18.350
It could be as complex as
creating a full launcher,

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:20.690
such that, really,
it almost feels

00:05:20.690 --> 00:05:24.170
like the ticket kiosks you might
see here at the AMC theater.

00:05:24.170 --> 00:05:26.450
I only see those
particular workflows.

00:05:26.450 --> 00:05:29.090
I don't see anything
else on that device.

00:05:29.090 --> 00:05:32.070
And you can optionally, on
those fully manage devices,

00:05:32.070 --> 00:05:35.300
if you're using a corporate
liable as your mechanism

00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:37.460
of ownership, so
you own the device,

00:05:37.460 --> 00:05:40.520
you want to have a high degree
of control, most recently,

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:43.220
with Android 8, we allow
you to deploy a work profile

00:05:43.220 --> 00:05:44.910
on those fully managed devices.

00:05:44.910 --> 00:05:46.590
And what that
means is, you still

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:48.590
have that high degree of
control over the assets

00:05:48.590 --> 00:05:51.020
you own but the end
user can go and bring

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:52.880
their own apps to
work, and there's still

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:55.340
that high degree of data
separation between the two.

00:05:55.340 --> 00:05:56.690
So a ton of flexibility.

00:05:56.690 --> 00:06:02.390
Virtually every use case that we
have encountered over the years

00:06:02.390 --> 00:06:04.730
has been now tackled inside
of the Android Enterprise

00:06:04.730 --> 00:06:06.140
framework.

00:06:06.140 --> 00:06:08.840
We've also done a ton of
investment into security.

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:11.570
We believe that,
ultimately, Android

00:06:11.570 --> 00:06:13.880
has to have a high
degree of security.

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:17.360
And that starts with
the operating system.

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:21.920
We really have a lot of
security tools baked in,

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:24.620
things like encryption,
things like secure boot,

00:06:24.620 --> 00:06:26.420
so we actually validate
that the operating

00:06:26.420 --> 00:06:28.340
system has booted properly.

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:30.470
We're doing things that
integrate the operating

00:06:30.470 --> 00:06:31.550
system into the hardware.

00:06:31.550 --> 00:06:35.210
So when you call cryptographic
functions in Android,

00:06:35.210 --> 00:06:38.240
those keys get stored in
a dedicated secure element

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:39.660
on that device.

00:06:39.660 --> 00:06:42.820
And we also are dedicating
the secure boot process

00:06:42.820 --> 00:06:43.950
out to that secure element.

00:06:43.950 --> 00:06:46.580
So it's a high
degree of assurance

00:06:46.580 --> 00:06:48.590
that that device
hasn't been tampered

00:06:48.590 --> 00:06:51.020
with before it interacts with
any of the corporate data

00:06:51.020 --> 00:06:53.300
that you might
decide to put on it.

00:06:53.300 --> 00:06:55.010
We have a lot of
management APIs that

00:06:55.010 --> 00:06:57.350
allow you to easily
customize and tailor

00:06:57.350 --> 00:07:00.410
and provision that
device experience.

00:07:00.410 --> 00:07:03.200
And we hook all of this
up to Google Play Protect.

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:05.240
So Google Play
Protect, how many folks

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:07.590
are familiar with
Google Play Protect?

00:07:07.590 --> 00:07:08.490
Few people?

00:07:08.490 --> 00:07:11.250
For the rest of you that
aren't familiar with it,

00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:12.750
Google Play Protect
is actually what

00:07:12.750 --> 00:07:14.880
we're doing here at
Google to tackle malware

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:19.530
on Android as well as on
Android VMs in Chrome.

00:07:19.530 --> 00:07:22.110
What we do is we scan
every application

00:07:22.110 --> 00:07:23.490
that is in Google Play.

00:07:23.490 --> 00:07:26.460
We are actually using our
web search technologies

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:28.680
to go and look out
to the broader web

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:31.230
and find applications that are
hosted outside of Google Play

00:07:31.230 --> 00:07:32.830
and we scan those as well.

00:07:32.830 --> 00:07:35.860
We can optionally, if the
user is opted into this,

00:07:35.860 --> 00:07:38.220
look at the device itself,
pull applications off

00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:39.810
of it that may have
been side loaded

00:07:39.810 --> 00:07:41.123
and validate those as well.

00:07:41.123 --> 00:07:42.540
And what that means
is, every day,

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:46.290
we're scanning, give or take, 50
billion different applications

00:07:46.290 --> 00:07:48.850
to flag for what
those applications are

00:07:48.850 --> 00:07:50.940
and whether they can
be potentially harmful.

00:07:50.940 --> 00:07:52.770
There's always
work to do on this,

00:07:52.770 --> 00:07:55.500
but since we
launched this program

00:07:55.500 --> 00:07:57.960
it is less likely that you
will die in your bathtub

00:07:57.960 --> 00:08:00.142
than find malware
in Google Play.

00:08:00.142 --> 00:08:01.850
It used to be that we
use a less likely--

00:08:01.850 --> 00:08:03.870
to continue with the
morbidity thread--

00:08:03.870 --> 00:08:06.623
that you'd be less likely
to get hit by lightning.

00:08:06.623 --> 00:08:08.040
But we've since
actually broadened

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:09.123
our definition of malware.

00:08:09.123 --> 00:08:12.630
We're hunting for more things
here at Google in order

00:08:12.630 --> 00:08:14.570
to create a safe
environment for end users.

00:08:14.570 --> 00:08:17.623
And as we go and look for
more broader sets of things

00:08:17.623 --> 00:08:19.290
that are potentially
harmful, obviously,

00:08:19.290 --> 00:08:21.700
we're cracking down as well.

00:08:21.700 --> 00:08:25.290
A lot of the data that we look
at from a security perspective

00:08:25.290 --> 00:08:27.990
is also exposed out
to you as developers.

00:08:27.990 --> 00:08:31.330
There's an entire suite inside
Android called SafetyNet,

00:08:31.330 --> 00:08:33.780
and that allows you to
query for things like,

00:08:33.780 --> 00:08:35.429
did the device boot properly?

00:08:35.429 --> 00:08:38.580
Has Google Play Protect
found malware on this device?

00:08:38.580 --> 00:08:42.888
Are my cryptographic keys
still not been tampered with?

00:08:42.888 --> 00:08:45.180
And so you can actually have
a high degree of certainty

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:46.920
certainly through
your management APIs

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:49.800
but also you as developers can
actually query for that data

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:52.200
to make sure that the
device is in a secure state

00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:55.173
before any data is handled.

00:08:55.173 --> 00:08:57.090
Now, I like to think
from a Google perspective

00:08:57.090 --> 00:08:59.007
we're doing quite well,
but it's always better

00:08:59.007 --> 00:09:01.100
to have validation
from third parties.

00:09:01.100 --> 00:09:04.080
Gartner has gone through
and done a recent analysis

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:07.260
of various mobile
operating systems that

00:09:07.260 --> 00:09:09.030
are in the market--

00:09:09.030 --> 00:09:11.370
Android and others.

00:09:11.370 --> 00:09:15.570
And the key thing to
look at in this chart

00:09:15.570 --> 00:09:18.810
are Android 8 and
then Android on Pixel,

00:09:18.810 --> 00:09:21.537
which is sort of a very
pure version of Android.

00:09:21.537 --> 00:09:23.370
And the key thing also
to know in this chart

00:09:23.370 --> 00:09:25.090
is that dark blue is better.

00:09:25.090 --> 00:09:27.450
And so in Gartner's own
analysis-- and there's

00:09:27.450 --> 00:09:30.900
a whole video up on
YouTube that will walk us

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:32.970
through how this
analysis was done,

00:09:32.970 --> 00:09:35.730
talks to Dave Kleidermacher,
our VP of security.

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:37.620
But the point here
is that Gartner,

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:40.140
in this objective
analysis, found

00:09:40.140 --> 00:09:42.300
that Android has a
higher degree of security

00:09:42.300 --> 00:09:44.640
and deeper level of
feature integration

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:49.730
from a security perspective than
other platforms in the market.

00:09:49.730 --> 00:09:52.180
So with the security
issue tackled,

00:09:52.180 --> 00:09:54.070
or certainly an ongoing
thing that we've

00:09:54.070 --> 00:09:58.240
tackled, one question we
wanted to look at Mobile World

00:09:58.240 --> 00:10:01.000
Congress two years
ago was, how do we

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:03.670
make it easier for you all
to procure devices and, more

00:10:03.670 --> 00:10:06.783
importantly, get those
devices under management?

00:10:06.783 --> 00:10:08.200
We know that at
the end of the day

00:10:08.200 --> 00:10:10.420
business transformation
happens through apps,

00:10:10.420 --> 00:10:12.760
and we want to make sure
that these devices can

00:10:12.760 --> 00:10:17.680
be easily unboxed and
very easily be enrolled

00:10:17.680 --> 00:10:19.850
into whatever mobility
management toolset

00:10:19.850 --> 00:10:24.250
you're using so you can leverage
tools like Manage Google Play.

00:10:24.250 --> 00:10:30.130
And in the last year, we've
seen over 50 different resellers

00:10:30.130 --> 00:10:31.870
join the zero-touch program.

00:10:31.870 --> 00:10:34.450
These are resellers that can
actually sell you a device

00:10:34.450 --> 00:10:37.180
and actually activate
it through zero-touch.

00:10:37.180 --> 00:10:41.050
30 of those joined in just
second half of 2018 alone.

00:10:41.050 --> 00:10:43.390
So this is a program that
we continue to expand

00:10:43.390 --> 00:10:45.910
and we want to make sure that
we're working with the leading

00:10:45.910 --> 00:10:49.233
resellers that you all
rely on to make sure

00:10:49.233 --> 00:10:51.400
that you have this great
experience from the Android

00:10:51.400 --> 00:10:51.900
platform.

00:10:54.430 --> 00:10:56.820
So with all the
things that we're

00:10:56.820 --> 00:10:58.660
doing on the platform
side of things,

00:10:58.660 --> 00:11:01.140
and with all the things we're
doing around zero-touch,

00:11:01.140 --> 00:11:03.810
the question we get
from customers is,

00:11:03.810 --> 00:11:06.190
how do I know it's
enterprise ready?

00:11:06.190 --> 00:11:08.220
I mentioned to you
that back in Android 5

00:11:08.220 --> 00:11:10.380
is when we started our
technological investment

00:11:10.380 --> 00:11:11.280
on Android.

00:11:11.280 --> 00:11:13.900
But in Android 6 is where
we made it mandatory.

00:11:13.900 --> 00:11:18.420
We actually mandated
that developers and OEMs

00:11:18.420 --> 00:11:20.280
who were building
Android devices,

00:11:20.280 --> 00:11:23.370
they had to adopt the
Android Enterprise framework

00:11:23.370 --> 00:11:25.300
as a part of what
we call the CDD--

00:11:25.300 --> 00:11:27.450
the compatibility
definition document.

00:11:27.450 --> 00:11:30.420
And what that
basically means is,

00:11:30.420 --> 00:11:32.763
devices will come with
Android Enterprise

00:11:32.763 --> 00:11:33.930
as a part of that framework.

00:11:33.930 --> 00:11:37.590
In fact, you can go through
and buy a $50 Android Go

00:11:37.590 --> 00:11:41.520
device in India unlocked,
and it will be enrollable

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:43.380
into a mobility
management system,

00:11:43.380 --> 00:11:46.110
like Google Cloud
Identity Manager.

00:11:46.110 --> 00:11:47.857
Do I think that
that $50 Android Go

00:11:47.857 --> 00:11:49.440
device is what you
should be deploying

00:11:49.440 --> 00:11:50.730
to all of your employees?

00:11:50.730 --> 00:11:51.927
Probably not.

00:11:51.927 --> 00:11:53.760
There's a lot of
trade-offs with Android Go,

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:56.070
including around things
like horsepower and memory

00:11:56.070 --> 00:11:57.960
to get to that $50 price point.

00:11:57.960 --> 00:12:01.020
In fact, the Android team
has built an entire suite

00:12:01.020 --> 00:12:04.570
of Go apps that are optimized
for these really low bandwidth,

00:12:04.570 --> 00:12:05.970
low memory environments.

00:12:05.970 --> 00:12:08.490
And the likelihood that
your Enterprise apps,

00:12:08.490 --> 00:12:10.530
like SAP and like
Salesforce, will

00:12:10.530 --> 00:12:14.340
run as well as you would
expect, is probably pretty low.

00:12:14.340 --> 00:12:17.540
So with so much availability
on the Android platform

00:12:17.540 --> 00:12:20.930
being out there, we really
wanted to answer this question

00:12:20.930 --> 00:12:23.240
and help our customers
answer this question, which

00:12:23.240 --> 00:12:26.150
is, what can I buy
in the market that

00:12:26.150 --> 00:12:30.180
is going to be Enterprise class,
not just Enterprise ready?

00:12:30.180 --> 00:12:34.160
And so we thought about this
from a technical perspective,

00:12:34.160 --> 00:12:38.150
working with our customers,
we identified three core areas

00:12:38.150 --> 00:12:40.500
that they brought up to us.

00:12:40.500 --> 00:12:42.180
One was confidence
and selection.

00:12:42.180 --> 00:12:46.010
So what they were asking
us was, if I buy a device

00:12:46.010 --> 00:12:48.650
and I'm particularly a
multinational organization--

00:12:48.650 --> 00:12:50.440
any multinationals here?

00:12:50.440 --> 00:12:51.950
A few of you?

00:12:51.950 --> 00:12:55.460
So are those devices going to be
available on a worldwide basis?

00:12:55.460 --> 00:12:59.420
Can I buy them unlocked and
allow my employee in London,

00:12:59.420 --> 00:13:04.490
my employee in New York,
and my employee in Singapore

00:13:04.490 --> 00:13:07.400
to get that device and
have that device run

00:13:07.400 --> 00:13:09.660
inside of my organization?

00:13:09.660 --> 00:13:11.870
The second is consistency
and deployment.

00:13:11.870 --> 00:13:15.110
What we wanted to make sure
was that end users, when they

00:13:15.110 --> 00:13:17.690
enroll their devices to get
access to corporate data

00:13:17.690 --> 00:13:19.550
and get access to
applications, we're

00:13:19.550 --> 00:13:21.270
having a consistent experience.

00:13:21.270 --> 00:13:23.840
Now, this is not
saying that we want

00:13:23.840 --> 00:13:27.860
to prevent the OEMs from
innovating on top of UX.

00:13:27.860 --> 00:13:30.530
We don't want-- the Huaweis
of the world, as an example--

00:13:30.530 --> 00:13:33.400
to stop using their
UI frameworks.

00:13:33.400 --> 00:13:34.883
What we do want
to do is make sure

00:13:34.883 --> 00:13:36.800
that the enrollment
workflows and the settings

00:13:36.800 --> 00:13:39.260
workflows to get connected
into your enterprise

00:13:39.260 --> 00:13:40.440
were largely consistent.

00:13:40.440 --> 00:13:42.110
And that's what
we've been working on

00:13:42.110 --> 00:13:44.330
with our OEM partners.

00:13:44.330 --> 00:13:46.520
And finally, we wanted
to make sure that devices

00:13:46.520 --> 00:13:47.480
were current and up-to-date.

00:13:47.480 --> 00:13:48.980
We wanted to make
sure that when you

00:13:48.980 --> 00:13:52.970
buy a device, that these devices
were going to get patches

00:13:52.970 --> 00:13:56.390
at least once every 90 days,
and that they would be getting

00:13:56.390 --> 00:14:00.400
those patches for a
minimum time period,

00:14:00.400 --> 00:14:03.470
and that those devices would get
at least one dessert upgrade.

00:14:03.470 --> 00:14:05.630
What we call a dessert
upgrade here at Google

00:14:05.630 --> 00:14:08.360
is a major feature
update of Android.

00:14:08.360 --> 00:14:11.340
And what that means is,
going from, as an example,

00:14:11.340 --> 00:14:14.940
Android 8 Oreo to Android 9 Pie.

00:14:14.940 --> 00:14:17.930
The desserts are in the names.

00:14:17.930 --> 00:14:19.760
And so those were the
really three criteria

00:14:19.760 --> 00:14:22.470
that we saw from customers.

00:14:22.470 --> 00:14:25.220
We then went to the over
200 OEMs that we worked with

00:14:25.220 --> 00:14:28.820
and we asked them, who would
like to join this program?

00:14:28.820 --> 00:14:32.090
Who is going to sign up to
this elevated set of criteria?

00:14:32.090 --> 00:14:34.370
And who is going to go
and also commit, frankly,

00:14:34.370 --> 00:14:36.962
to things like
training, making sure

00:14:36.962 --> 00:14:39.170
that they understand Android
and they can position it

00:14:39.170 --> 00:14:41.210
well to their customers?

00:14:41.210 --> 00:14:44.630
And since then, we've
had 12 OEMs bring over

00:14:44.630 --> 00:14:46.850
60 different devices
to market that are

00:14:46.850 --> 00:14:50.160
part of the Android Enterprise
Recommended portfolio.

00:14:50.160 --> 00:14:53.090
These devices, a lot of the
ones I decided to throw up here

00:14:53.090 --> 00:14:55.205
on the slide, are
flagship devices,

00:14:55.205 --> 00:14:57.080
just because those are
the shiniest ones that

00:14:57.080 --> 00:14:58.620
are out there.

00:14:58.620 --> 00:15:02.750
But it also represents a lot
of different price points.

00:15:02.750 --> 00:15:07.430
You can now go and work with the
likes of Nokia, Huawei, and LG

00:15:07.430 --> 00:15:12.740
and find devices that are at
that $150 to $300 price point

00:15:12.740 --> 00:15:15.260
so you can support things like
corporate liable deployments

00:15:15.260 --> 00:15:15.860
of Androids.

00:15:15.860 --> 00:15:18.960
This is not just a
program targeted at $700

00:15:18.960 --> 00:15:20.490
and up from a
flagship perspective.

00:15:20.490 --> 00:15:24.740
It really is every price
point along that graph.

00:15:24.740 --> 00:15:26.500
And so we're seeing
a lot of success.

00:15:26.500 --> 00:15:28.958
I'll talk about that in a minute
of just customers starting

00:15:28.958 --> 00:15:30.560
to really think
about this program

00:15:30.560 --> 00:15:33.720
and using it as a way to
help them procure devices

00:15:33.720 --> 00:15:35.260
more easily.

00:15:35.260 --> 00:15:38.730
But we got a bit
of a poke, which

00:15:38.730 --> 00:15:41.860
was, that's great for
knowledge workers,

00:15:41.860 --> 00:15:43.500
but what if I'm a Walmart?

00:15:43.500 --> 00:15:48.210
What if I'm using Android
across my entire organization,

00:15:48.210 --> 00:15:52.200
from the logistics warehouse all
the way out to the store front?

00:15:52.200 --> 00:15:55.950
Those devices, as great
as the Google Pixel 3 is,

00:15:55.950 --> 00:15:58.740
I've seen videos [INAUDIBLE]
did a really cool case

00:15:58.740 --> 00:16:01.350
study with Walmart
and material design,

00:16:01.350 --> 00:16:04.170
looking at how those devices
are used in the warehouse.

00:16:04.170 --> 00:16:05.860
The Pixel 3 is
probably not going

00:16:05.860 --> 00:16:08.340
to go and hold up to the
abuse that a guy who's

00:16:08.340 --> 00:16:10.260
trying to stack
pallets at Walmart

00:16:10.260 --> 00:16:12.360
is going to go and put on it.

00:16:12.360 --> 00:16:16.380
And so the good news is that
Android's a very open platform.

00:16:16.380 --> 00:16:19.050
We work with a lot
of different OEMs

00:16:19.050 --> 00:16:21.450
to build really unique
devices, devices

00:16:21.450 --> 00:16:25.050
that are really built to suit
for a lot of different use

00:16:25.050 --> 00:16:26.160
cases.

00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:29.820
And one of those use cases
is the ruggedized market.

00:16:29.820 --> 00:16:34.380
IDC has, in a few different
research publications,

00:16:34.380 --> 00:16:37.440
highlighted that Android
is the next logical choice

00:16:37.440 --> 00:16:40.680
for the rugged market, as
Microsoft has end-of-lifed

00:16:40.680 --> 00:16:42.487
the Windows CE platform.

00:16:42.487 --> 00:16:44.070
And we're really
excited to be working

00:16:44.070 --> 00:16:50.010
with leading OEMs, like Zebra,
like Honeywell, like Panasonic,

00:16:50.010 --> 00:16:52.890
and Sonim, Point Mobile,
Datalogic, Moto, and Motorola

00:16:52.890 --> 00:16:54.210
Solutions, and Bluebird.

00:16:54.210 --> 00:16:58.380
This really represents many
of the purpose-built OEMs

00:16:58.380 --> 00:17:00.880
and ruggedized
OEMs in the market.

00:17:00.880 --> 00:17:03.323
And when we built the criteria
for Enterprise Recommended

00:17:03.323 --> 00:17:04.740
for rugged, we
knew that was going

00:17:04.740 --> 00:17:07.079
to be different than what
we should be thinking

00:17:07.079 --> 00:17:08.970
about for knowledge workers.

00:17:08.970 --> 00:17:12.630
We wanted to make sure that
Enterprise Recommended was

00:17:12.630 --> 00:17:15.810
tackling things
like drop testing,

00:17:15.810 --> 00:17:19.230
tackling things like dust
intrusion and water intrusion.

00:17:19.230 --> 00:17:22.500
In fact, if you go down to
the show floor later today

00:17:22.500 --> 00:17:24.900
or tomorrow, we have
many of these devices

00:17:24.900 --> 00:17:29.820
up in the Android
Enterprise Recommended area

00:17:29.820 --> 00:17:31.988
in the workplace
transformation pavilion.

00:17:31.988 --> 00:17:34.280
So you can get a feel for
what those devices look like.

00:17:34.280 --> 00:17:37.470
So what's important to note
here is that this is not just

00:17:37.470 --> 00:17:40.800
us going to the market
and saying, hey, sign up

00:17:40.800 --> 00:17:42.260
and we'll slap a badge on it.

00:17:42.260 --> 00:17:45.570
We're actually validating
that these devices

00:17:45.570 --> 00:17:46.950
are meeting these criteria.

00:17:46.950 --> 00:17:50.430
We're looking at those
workflows for enrollment

00:17:50.430 --> 00:17:52.980
and making sure that
those devices are

00:17:52.980 --> 00:17:55.860
doing the things that are part
of the Enterprise Recommended

00:17:55.860 --> 00:17:56.820
criteria.

00:17:56.820 --> 00:17:59.730
We're going through
and looking at how

00:17:59.730 --> 00:18:04.870
these devices act when they're
under enterprise management.

00:18:04.870 --> 00:18:07.180
We're not drop-testing devices.

00:18:07.180 --> 00:18:09.240
That is an area where
we look to the partners

00:18:09.240 --> 00:18:11.280
to show us validation
around things

00:18:11.280 --> 00:18:13.110
like IP ratings and the like.

00:18:13.110 --> 00:18:15.480
But we are actually
independently looking

00:18:15.480 --> 00:18:19.290
at these devices to ensure
they're meeting our criteria.

00:18:19.290 --> 00:18:24.240
And so far, we've seen a lot
of really positive reaction

00:18:24.240 --> 00:18:25.650
from the market.

00:18:25.650 --> 00:18:30.740
Data from HMD Global, which is
a manufacturer of Nokia devices,

00:18:30.740 --> 00:18:32.360
has found that many--

00:18:32.360 --> 00:18:37.260
and over 56% of folks are aware
of the Enterprise Recommended

00:18:37.260 --> 00:18:37.780
program.

00:18:37.780 --> 00:18:40.290
But more importantly, they're
really looking to the program

00:18:40.290 --> 00:18:41.910
to make purchasing decisions.

00:18:41.910 --> 00:18:46.830
And data from a recent
survey in Europe from IDC

00:18:46.830 --> 00:18:51.030
said that, I think as a result
of a lot of the security

00:18:51.030 --> 00:18:53.580
investment we've been
putting into Android,

00:18:53.580 --> 00:18:56.595
they believe that those devices
that have been validated

00:18:56.595 --> 00:18:59.220
by Google and that are using the
latest and greatest of Android

00:18:59.220 --> 00:19:03.010
actually can be more
secure than iOS.

00:19:03.010 --> 00:19:06.850
And we're seeing a variety of
different organizations really

00:19:06.850 --> 00:19:09.400
look toward Enterprise
Recommended as their way

00:19:09.400 --> 00:19:12.850
of supporting or the Android
platform inside of their IT

00:19:12.850 --> 00:19:15.490
organization.

00:19:15.490 --> 00:19:19.900
HSBC is one of those, and
HSBC has come out publicly

00:19:19.900 --> 00:19:22.150
and stated that they will
only look toward Enterprise

00:19:22.150 --> 00:19:26.560
Recommended devices as they
send out requests for proposals

00:19:26.560 --> 00:19:29.830
for corporate liable, as they
think about their BYOD catalog

00:19:29.830 --> 00:19:31.450
inside the bank.

00:19:31.450 --> 00:19:33.670
The notable point also
is that Android is really

00:19:33.670 --> 00:19:37.810
being proven across those most
sensitive verticals, places

00:19:37.810 --> 00:19:41.890
like financial services,
and government,

00:19:41.890 --> 00:19:44.300
and health care, and the like.

00:19:44.300 --> 00:19:47.890
SAP is another partner
that has actually come

00:19:47.890 --> 00:19:52.450
out very publicly and said
that Enterprise Recommended was

00:19:52.450 --> 00:19:58.120
a key initiative for them,
and specifically for BYOD.

00:19:58.120 --> 00:20:02.590
A lot of people think about
BYOD as bring your own device.

00:20:02.590 --> 00:20:04.170
And I know I'm not
in the recording.

00:20:04.170 --> 00:20:07.450
I am widening my arms
as wide as I can.

00:20:07.450 --> 00:20:10.570
But the idea here is that
the challenge with that

00:20:10.570 --> 00:20:13.630
is, can IT assure
that these devices are

00:20:13.630 --> 00:20:16.330
going to actually be
secure and be handling

00:20:16.330 --> 00:20:17.330
corporate data properly?

00:20:17.330 --> 00:20:20.470
And so many
organizations, like SAP

00:20:20.470 --> 00:20:24.010
and like HSBC, when they
start their BYOD programs,

00:20:24.010 --> 00:20:25.590
they really say,
here are the catalog

00:20:25.590 --> 00:20:28.540
of devices we will allow
into our organization.

00:20:28.540 --> 00:20:30.620
BYOD doesn't mean bring
your own everything.

00:20:30.620 --> 00:20:32.470
It means, if you want
to support Android,

00:20:32.470 --> 00:20:35.230
here are the Android devices
we're willing to let in.

00:20:35.230 --> 00:20:37.020
If we want to support
other platforms,

00:20:37.020 --> 00:20:39.500
here is the catalog that
you can choose from.

00:20:39.500 --> 00:20:41.830
And we really see
a lot of success

00:20:41.830 --> 00:20:46.210
from our customers when they
use that method to launch

00:20:46.210 --> 00:20:48.400
their BYOD initiatives
inside of their company.

00:20:51.370 --> 00:20:55.250
So I mentioned it's really
around these three criteria.

00:20:55.250 --> 00:20:59.350
But as we've rolled out the
hardware program early last

00:20:59.350 --> 00:21:02.440
year-- or I guess almost
two years ago now--

00:21:02.440 --> 00:21:05.950
we heard from our customers
that these were really

00:21:05.950 --> 00:21:08.950
good initiatives to helping
us figure out hardware.

00:21:08.950 --> 00:21:10.780
But hardware is
not the be all end

00:21:10.780 --> 00:21:12.290
all of a mobility deployment.

00:21:12.290 --> 00:21:14.590
There's a lot of
cases where there's

00:21:14.590 --> 00:21:18.670
additional services, additional
support, additional software

00:21:18.670 --> 00:21:20.990
that I might want to roll
out into my organization.

00:21:20.990 --> 00:21:22.680
How do I know that
those are going

00:21:22.680 --> 00:21:25.330
to be meeting a
similar set of criteria

00:21:25.330 --> 00:21:28.990
and will be successful inside
of my mobility deployment?

00:21:28.990 --> 00:21:32.730
And so, this year, we launched
Android Enterprise Recommended

00:21:32.730 --> 00:21:34.620
services, which was
really in response

00:21:34.620 --> 00:21:38.280
to what we were hearing from
customers around validating

00:21:38.280 --> 00:21:41.670
the broader set of ecosystem
that Android supports

00:21:41.670 --> 00:21:42.840
beyond just hardware.

00:21:42.840 --> 00:21:45.220
And we focused on
two core areas.

00:21:45.220 --> 00:21:47.040
One is enterprise
mobility management,

00:21:47.040 --> 00:21:51.060
so the suite of services that
are managing devices inside

00:21:51.060 --> 00:21:54.370
of your organization, as well
as managed service providers.

00:21:54.370 --> 00:21:58.680
So this is the actual partner,
the technical partners,

00:21:58.680 --> 00:22:02.340
who may be offering deployment
and support services

00:22:02.340 --> 00:22:05.770
to assist your IT departments.

00:22:05.770 --> 00:22:10.000
The EMM requirements really
focused on these four key areas

00:22:10.000 --> 00:22:12.670
on the slide, but
one was making sure

00:22:12.670 --> 00:22:16.220
that EMMs were stepping up to
the variety of different ways

00:22:16.220 --> 00:22:17.950
the Android can be deployed.

00:22:17.950 --> 00:22:19.820
When an EMM partner
works with Google,

00:22:19.820 --> 00:22:22.510
we actually do
validate the solution

00:22:22.510 --> 00:22:27.490
sets, whether it's fully
managed, work profile,

00:22:27.490 --> 00:22:29.110
dedicated.

00:22:29.110 --> 00:22:32.080
We validate that those
particular software

00:22:32.080 --> 00:22:34.780
implementations, whether they're
Enterprise Recommended or not,

00:22:34.780 --> 00:22:37.870
correspond to the technical
specs that we author.

00:22:37.870 --> 00:22:40.900
But what we were finding
is that not every EMM

00:22:40.900 --> 00:22:42.910
offered every deployment style.

00:22:42.910 --> 00:22:45.790
At the same time, IT was
trying to go through and use

00:22:45.790 --> 00:22:49.240
Android in a variety of
different deployment scenarios.

00:22:49.240 --> 00:22:51.040
I mentioned Walmart
as an example.

00:22:51.040 --> 00:22:53.890
You can imagine how you
deploy a device at Walmart

00:22:53.890 --> 00:22:57.730
or at Marks and Spencer
on the store floor

00:22:57.730 --> 00:23:00.190
is very different than
how you might deploy

00:23:00.190 --> 00:23:02.530
Android inside the
accounting department

00:23:02.530 --> 00:23:04.600
or the other parts of
the carpeted enterprise

00:23:04.600 --> 00:23:06.650
inside of those organizations.

00:23:06.650 --> 00:23:08.350
And so we really
wanted to validate

00:23:08.350 --> 00:23:11.048
that advanced feature
sets from at least two

00:23:11.048 --> 00:23:13.090
of the different deployment
mechanisms of Android

00:23:13.090 --> 00:23:15.490
Enterprise.

00:23:15.490 --> 00:23:19.020
Two of the three were supported.

00:23:19.020 --> 00:23:22.470
We wanted to make sure also that
these partners were offering

00:23:22.470 --> 00:23:24.450
dedicated technical leadership.

00:23:24.450 --> 00:23:27.510
I actually came from MobileIron
before I joined Google.

00:23:27.510 --> 00:23:30.300
In many cases, EMMs
act as the front line

00:23:30.300 --> 00:23:32.550
of support for many
mobility deployments

00:23:32.550 --> 00:23:35.700
because it's the glue
that's holding things

00:23:35.700 --> 00:23:39.360
together and actually activating
the deployment devices

00:23:39.360 --> 00:23:41.280
inside of corporate IT.

00:23:41.280 --> 00:23:44.310
And so we wanted to make sure
that these organizations did

00:23:44.310 --> 00:23:45.100
two things.

00:23:45.100 --> 00:23:48.570
One was that, in
managing Android,

00:23:48.570 --> 00:23:51.750
Android Enterprise would be
the default and primary mode

00:23:51.750 --> 00:23:55.200
of management that
these pieces of software

00:23:55.200 --> 00:23:57.600
would be using inside
of deployments.

00:23:57.600 --> 00:24:01.650
And two, that there was guidance
and documentation for customers

00:24:01.650 --> 00:24:03.690
that were either
rolling out or migrating

00:24:03.690 --> 00:24:05.010
to Android Enterprise.

00:24:05.010 --> 00:24:08.460
We really wanted to make sure
that best practices from Google

00:24:08.460 --> 00:24:10.920
were well-established
not just from ourselves

00:24:10.920 --> 00:24:12.960
but from these
technical partners that

00:24:12.960 --> 00:24:16.650
are going out and helping
customers deploy Android

00:24:16.650 --> 00:24:18.703
inside of their enterprise.

00:24:18.703 --> 00:24:20.620
We wanted to make sure
that they were offering

00:24:20.620 --> 00:24:22.360
Enterprise-level support.

00:24:22.360 --> 00:24:25.240
So we've actually been
doing a lot of training

00:24:25.240 --> 00:24:27.940
from Google of these
partners to make sure

00:24:27.940 --> 00:24:31.570
that if you call a given
EMM, they understand--

00:24:31.570 --> 00:24:34.145
at least their escalation
teams will well understand

00:24:34.145 --> 00:24:35.020
the Android platform.

00:24:35.020 --> 00:24:37.750
They can troubleshoot how
Androids deployed inside

00:24:37.750 --> 00:24:38.878
of an organization.

00:24:38.878 --> 00:24:40.420
And finally, we
really wanted to make

00:24:40.420 --> 00:24:42.910
sure there was a proven
ability to deploy,

00:24:42.910 --> 00:24:46.270
that these particular partners
had worked with large IT

00:24:46.270 --> 00:24:48.940
organizations, had demonstrable
success with these IT

00:24:48.940 --> 00:24:52.750
organizations, and frankly
made sure that their field

00:24:52.750 --> 00:24:55.180
organizations-- their
sales and technical sales

00:24:55.180 --> 00:24:58.420
specialists-- were
well-trained by Google

00:24:58.420 --> 00:25:01.270
about Android Enterprise so
that the relevant technical

00:25:01.270 --> 00:25:05.200
knowledge was being
conveyed to the customer.

00:25:05.200 --> 00:25:07.840
We're really excited
by the traction

00:25:07.840 --> 00:25:11.620
we're getting in the early
days of Enterprise Recommended.

00:25:11.620 --> 00:25:14.842
We've had so far nine
partners join this program.

00:25:14.842 --> 00:25:16.300
Many of these are
the ones that are

00:25:16.300 --> 00:25:20.420
in the top right of
various market surveys,

00:25:20.420 --> 00:25:23.980
whether they be from
Forrester, Gartner, or IDC.

00:25:23.980 --> 00:25:27.040
And we really are
pleased with the level

00:25:27.040 --> 00:25:28.540
of technical
leadership that they're

00:25:28.540 --> 00:25:37.150
bringing to the table in support
of your enterprise mobility

00:25:37.150 --> 00:25:40.070
deployments.

00:25:40.070 --> 00:25:42.630
The second area was around
managed service providers.

00:25:42.630 --> 00:25:44.840
There's a lot of
organizations, particularly

00:25:44.840 --> 00:25:46.970
if you look at the
desktop world, that

00:25:46.970 --> 00:25:49.370
have decided to outsource
frontline support

00:25:49.370 --> 00:25:52.790
or outsource the deployment
services for platforms

00:25:52.790 --> 00:25:55.460
like Windows, Mac,
and even Chromebook.

00:25:55.460 --> 00:25:58.610
And so we wanted to make
sure, as organizations

00:25:58.610 --> 00:26:01.850
start to look toward their
mobility deployments being

00:26:01.850 --> 00:26:06.320
handled in the same way, that
managed services providers were

00:26:06.320 --> 00:26:08.330
really, really well
trained and offered

00:26:08.330 --> 00:26:12.350
a high degree of technical depth
in supporting your deployments.

00:26:12.350 --> 00:26:15.137
So again, it really falls
in these three core areas

00:26:15.137 --> 00:26:16.970
of confidence in
selection, that these folks

00:26:16.970 --> 00:26:18.950
are validated and
trained by Google,

00:26:18.950 --> 00:26:21.320
that they have proven
expertise, that they have

00:26:21.320 --> 00:26:23.780
consistent training and
standardized on escalation

00:26:23.780 --> 00:26:27.080
processes from Google,
and that they're always

00:26:27.080 --> 00:26:29.750
current on the latest
training requirements,

00:26:29.750 --> 00:26:31.880
and frankly, that they
routinely collaborate

00:26:31.880 --> 00:26:33.800
with us on business objectives.

00:26:33.800 --> 00:26:36.540
Android is obviously an
ever-evolving platform,

00:26:36.540 --> 00:26:39.200
and so we do want to make
sure that our partners, both

00:26:39.200 --> 00:26:43.090
the EMMs as well as the MSPs,
are continually keeping up

00:26:43.090 --> 00:26:45.590
to date in the services that
they're bringing to the market.

00:26:49.010 --> 00:26:54.020
From a deeper dive into where
that platform looks like,

00:26:54.020 --> 00:26:58.280
we mandate that 80% of the sales
and technical sales specialists

00:26:58.280 --> 00:27:00.560
are trained in what we
call Android Academy.

00:27:00.560 --> 00:27:06.140
So Android Academy is a program
we launched about 18 months

00:27:06.140 --> 00:27:08.510
ago, designed to make
sure that the best

00:27:08.510 --> 00:27:10.370
practices and
knowledge of Google

00:27:10.370 --> 00:27:11.760
is out there in the market.

00:27:11.760 --> 00:27:13.177
For those of you
that are curious,

00:27:13.177 --> 00:27:15.560
it's up at
AndroidEnterprise.training.

00:27:15.560 --> 00:27:18.480
And that is designed to
target toward two core areas.

00:27:18.480 --> 00:27:21.020
One is sales specialists,
that you understand

00:27:21.020 --> 00:27:24.690
how to do basic positioning
of the Android platform,

00:27:24.690 --> 00:27:27.830
that you understand
that Android is secure

00:27:27.830 --> 00:27:30.000
and that it can be used
to do really cool things

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:31.490
around business transformation.

00:27:31.490 --> 00:27:34.400
And then it dives
into a higher degree

00:27:34.400 --> 00:27:36.540
of technical depth for
technical sales specialists,

00:27:36.540 --> 00:27:41.450
so not just that Android is
secure but actually how Android

00:27:41.450 --> 00:27:43.610
offers that security,
how that security is

00:27:43.610 --> 00:27:46.100
integrated to the hardware
so you can position

00:27:46.100 --> 00:27:48.050
that data to your customer.

00:27:48.050 --> 00:27:51.050
We've since expanded
that program

00:27:51.050 --> 00:27:54.080
through an invite-only program
called the Android Enterprise

00:27:54.080 --> 00:27:55.520
Experts program.

00:27:55.520 --> 00:28:00.470
And that really takes a much
deeper set of validation

00:28:00.470 --> 00:28:01.365
to the individual.

00:28:01.365 --> 00:28:02.990
We're actually going
through-- and what

00:28:02.990 --> 00:28:04.730
we'll show you in a minute.

00:28:04.730 --> 00:28:06.740
I'll just show you now.

00:28:06.740 --> 00:28:08.840
We actually are taking
folks into the lab

00:28:08.840 --> 00:28:13.280
and we're making sure they know
how to troubleshoot something

00:28:13.280 --> 00:28:14.280
if something goes wrong.

00:28:14.280 --> 00:28:16.820
We introduced failure
scenarios that we've

00:28:16.820 --> 00:28:19.880
seen from actual
deployments and make sure

00:28:19.880 --> 00:28:22.640
that the support and
deployment engineers know

00:28:22.640 --> 00:28:25.410
how to troubleshoot through
these particular processes.

00:28:25.410 --> 00:28:28.490
And these MSP trainings
were run by Google.

00:28:28.490 --> 00:28:31.100
These were our partner and
customer engineering teams

00:28:31.100 --> 00:28:32.960
from Mountain View,
New York, and London,

00:28:32.960 --> 00:28:35.900
that traveled around
the world to train

00:28:35.900 --> 00:28:37.290
all of these individuals.

00:28:37.290 --> 00:28:39.620
And so when we
launched the program,

00:28:39.620 --> 00:28:46.370
mandated that the partners have
at least two Android Enterprise

00:28:46.370 --> 00:28:50.870
experts and two Android Support
engineers in each region

00:28:50.870 --> 00:28:53.340
that they serve.

00:28:53.340 --> 00:28:58.220
We also mandated that these
folks be trained on EMMs

00:28:58.220 --> 00:29:00.890
and that they actually
be trained-- and I think,

00:29:00.890 --> 00:29:02.930
I believe, at least two EMMs--

00:29:02.930 --> 00:29:07.130
and finally, that they have
demonstrative technical ability

00:29:07.130 --> 00:29:11.330
through customer deployments and
deploying Android Enterprise.

00:29:11.330 --> 00:29:13.580
Since we launched
the program, we've

00:29:13.580 --> 00:29:19.880
had some of the leading
organizations in Enterprise

00:29:19.880 --> 00:29:23.660
Mobility Services step up to
the plate and join this program.

00:29:23.660 --> 00:29:26.930
Leading brands like Accenture,
and Cognizant, and DXC,

00:29:26.930 --> 00:29:29.810
and Econocom, and
Honeywell have ponied up

00:29:29.810 --> 00:29:33.860
the technical resources
needed to ensure

00:29:33.860 --> 00:29:38.713
that they are offering great
services to the market.

00:29:38.713 --> 00:29:40.630
And I've been in mobility
for quite some time.

00:29:40.630 --> 00:29:43.130
I've very rarely
seen engineers get

00:29:43.130 --> 00:29:45.200
nervous in training programs.

00:29:45.200 --> 00:29:48.890
But the Android
Expert program was

00:29:48.890 --> 00:29:52.460
one of those where folks were
definitely put to the test,

00:29:52.460 --> 00:29:55.520
and we're super excited to
have these partners joining us.

00:29:58.200 --> 00:30:04.200
So when we [INAUDIBLE] get
this from a holistic level,

00:30:04.200 --> 00:30:08.790
we've really been investing
on a program-by-program basis

00:30:08.790 --> 00:30:12.060
to make sure that all of the
different partners that you

00:30:12.060 --> 00:30:17.160
work with are well served by
the best knowledge from Google,

00:30:17.160 --> 00:30:19.320
the best validation
of the services

00:30:19.320 --> 00:30:22.410
that these folks provide,
that the software we provide

00:30:22.410 --> 00:30:24.860
is being incorporated
into these product suites,

00:30:24.860 --> 00:30:27.390
and that this evolves over time.

00:30:27.390 --> 00:30:29.220
With every release
of Android, we're

00:30:29.220 --> 00:30:31.530
continuing to evolve the
technical requirements

00:30:31.530 --> 00:30:36.900
for devices, the training
requirements for EMMs and MSPs,

00:30:36.900 --> 00:30:40.380
and so this will be an evolving
program where you'll see,

00:30:40.380 --> 00:30:42.520
hopefully, new partners
join the program

00:30:42.520 --> 00:30:44.640
but certainly also
there's an assurance

00:30:44.640 --> 00:30:46.440
that these partners
are validated

00:30:46.440 --> 00:30:49.980
as new versions of
Android come out.

00:30:49.980 --> 00:30:53.290
I'll leave you with this.

00:30:53.290 --> 00:30:55.260
I asked the beginning
of this presentation,

00:30:55.260 --> 00:30:56.760
how many folks were
running Android,

00:30:56.760 --> 00:30:58.953
and lots of different
hands went up.

00:30:58.953 --> 00:31:01.620
And then I asked how many of you
were running Android Enterprise

00:31:01.620 --> 00:31:04.895
and not as many hands
went up, only a few.

00:31:04.895 --> 00:31:06.270
For those of you
that are running

00:31:06.270 --> 00:31:09.000
Android inside of your
organization, many of you

00:31:09.000 --> 00:31:11.970
may have been using
legacy software to do it.

00:31:11.970 --> 00:31:15.440
There was a suite of tools we
offered prior to Android 5,

00:31:15.440 --> 00:31:16.860
called Device Administrator.

00:31:16.860 --> 00:31:22.080
It offered basic client-side
APIs to set a device passcode,

00:31:22.080 --> 00:31:27.700
to turn off hardware like
Bluetooth or the camera.

00:31:27.700 --> 00:31:29.550
And these were
really basic tools,

00:31:29.550 --> 00:31:31.998
almost like the very
basic suite of policies

00:31:31.998 --> 00:31:34.290
that came with active sync
in the early days of Windows

00:31:34.290 --> 00:31:35.610
Mobile.

00:31:35.610 --> 00:31:39.660
We've since deprecated a lot
of those client-side APIs.

00:31:39.660 --> 00:31:44.790
In Android 9, we actually
deprecated passcode and device

00:31:44.790 --> 00:31:46.840
hardware controls
in documentation.

00:31:46.840 --> 00:31:49.800
So if you call those
APIs on Android 9,

00:31:49.800 --> 00:31:53.280
you'll get an error saying,
this isn't supported anymore.

00:31:53.280 --> 00:31:56.610
In Android 10, we're actually
going to turn those APIs off.

00:31:56.610 --> 00:31:59.310
So if your organization is
looking to actually support

00:31:59.310 --> 00:32:03.820
Android from an IT
management perspective,

00:32:03.820 --> 00:32:08.070
it's really important that
you be looking to the Android

00:32:08.070 --> 00:32:10.650
Enterprise framework to
actually roll this out

00:32:10.650 --> 00:32:12.240
in your organization.

00:32:12.240 --> 00:32:16.140
Up on our blog at blog.google in
the Android Enterprise product

00:32:16.140 --> 00:32:20.310
update section, there's a
blog post and a blue book

00:32:20.310 --> 00:32:22.560
that we've launched
to help you understand

00:32:22.560 --> 00:32:25.710
how to tackle that migration,
to take a cap and growth

00:32:25.710 --> 00:32:27.270
strategy to your
Android deployment

00:32:27.270 --> 00:32:30.390
to make sure that the
latest device is enrolling

00:32:30.390 --> 00:32:32.670
can actually work in the
Android Enterprise framework.

00:32:32.670 --> 00:32:34.240
And certainly, from
our perspective,

00:32:34.240 --> 00:32:37.080
we're making sure that
our mobility management

00:32:37.080 --> 00:32:39.480
partners and our
services partners

00:32:39.480 --> 00:32:41.570
are well trained to help
you through that journey.

00:32:41.570 --> 00:32:44.920
[MUSIC PLAYING]

